azgor schrieb:
> 
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net. Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.
> 
> [1]: Random forests, like in the Ranger quest. --SomeoneElseYes

Very powerful; you can get (at least?) one stack of fruit (or leaves)
out of every single tree. Maybe add a few more trees to fixed "open"
level layouts - Castle, Bigroom, Medusa's level, Oracle?

And for the totally anal folks, bananas might be removed as available
fruit, because what they grow on aren't, strictly speaking, trees ...

> [2]: Satchel of magic seeds;

That seems rather powerful to me. I'm all for redecorating the
dungeon, so if it weren't too abusable, i'd find it interesting. Maybe
restrict use so that a tree can only grow in a permanently-lit
location outside Gehennom and the Planes and won't produce fruit.

> [3]: Falling rock traps should be hallucinated as thwomp traps. "A
> thwomp falls on your head! THWOMP!

I feel like i'm missing an obscure pop culture reference here. If
there isn't one or if it's _really_ obscure, this'd be totally
pointless.

> [4]: Wind trap.

Snort. Absurd and pointless.

> [5]: Zapping a wand of nothing at intelligent monsters should scare
> them for a turn or two --Botje

No. That would be useful. If any such thing should happen, it should
work with _all_ wands.

> [6]: make cows and sheeps etc. --Zaba_

Not sufficiently dangerous. Better try goats.

> [7]: Yellow dragon scale mail should give petrification-resitance, like
> the dragon has. --SomeoneElseYes

No. The dragon is petrification-resistant, because it's acidic. The
armour already makes you acid-resistant, it doesn't make your blood
acidic. One power at a time, guv.

> [8]: Tourists #chatting with monsters can get directions
> (clairvoyance). Need to find a way of giving POOR directions from
> chaotic monsters though... --mathx

<snigger>
Tourists should get bad directions from everybody. Or perhaps i'm just
chaotic ...

> [9]: copper and silver coins in addition to gold --Zaba_

Not again.

> [10]: Ghosts' strength should reflect the character's level!
> --Chilliwack

Since most ghosts appear in graveyards and abandoned temples ...

No. The ghost is not supposed to be the guardian of the bonepile.

> [11]: A new monster, a trojan horse.

<picks self up from the floor>
Thanks for the laugh.

> [12]: Ice boxes should have a chance of holding potions of booze or
> fruit juice in addition to the random corpses. --Patashu

No. It's a meat locker, not a fridge.

> [13]: Eating a banana should leave a banana peel in your inventory.

And monkeys should have a random number from 2-10 of them. And there
should be public dustbins.

> [14]: Bolts of cold should freeze fountains. --BenUrban

And convert them into a frozen pool? Why not; i think fire attacks
already affect fountains.

> [15]: In some cases you might encounter the Fellowship of the Ring.

And if you hurt them, the armies of the West would descend on you!
Yikes. No. Way.

> [16]: An optional game ending: Rather than giving the Amulet to your
> god, you can instead opt to travel to ancient Yendor, which opens up a
> whole new realm of possibilities. Which are endless. --TheShrewdDude

Neat. When are you going to start coding?

> [17]: If your luck is in the negative, rooms are generated dark far
> more often. --Patashu

I don't think linking dungeon generation to character stats is a good
idea. Next thing someone'll suggest the likelihood of Ludios portals
and the selection of Sokoban levels should be affected by luck, or
charisma, or alignment.

> [19]: Messages should be printed when a monster's teleportitis kicks
> in.

Don't see why.

> [20]: Kicking stairs while hallucinating should have a rare (say,
> 1/256) chance of flipping them over to point up instead of down and
> down instead of up.

Don't see why.

> [21]: The big room should be generated with randomly scattered trees
> and either a lake or a river.

As one bigroom option, yes. As standard outfit, i don't think so.

> [22]: Rocs, thunderbirds and phoenixes. Because avians are cool.

And because avians just _love_ hanging around underground. No thanks.
Giant Rocs in Angband are stupid enough.

